# Build lane — close unauthenticated Elevate internal console

Date: 2026-10-18

## Result

No production configuration was changed. The target is reachable over the tailnet for HTTP, but host-shell access is denied by the Tailscale SSH policy for the only tested local account (`hd2admin`). Therefore I could not inspect the origin process, its configuration, or safely apply/verify an origin-side restriction. I did not guess a config path, firewall manager, or ACL policy.

## Findings

- This worker runs on `brandon-compute` as `hd2admin` (UID 1000).
- Route lookup for `100.69.211.43` selects `tailscale0` via source `100.106.6.22`.
- `http://100.69.211.43:8000/` is directly reachable and returns `HTTP/1.1 200 OK`, `Server: nginx`; there is no `WWW-Authenticate` response header. This establishes that Nginx answers the direct Tailscale-IP request, but not which local process/upstream or configuration file serves it.
- SSH port 22 is reachable, but Tailscale SSH returned: `tailscale: tailnet policy does not permit you to SSH as user "hd2admin"`. This is an authorization/policy block, not a network reachability failure.
- Public checks remain as required: agents root = 401; `/embed/` = 200; `/api/embed/` = 200; `https://elevatesci.com/` = 200. Existing public authentication was not changed.

## Access required / blocked work

A tailnet administrator must authorize an approved SSH identity for this host (or provide an approved privileged shell session), including `sudo` access if the chosen host-side correction requires it. Do not broaden this to an untrusted identity. Once access is granted, inspect the actual listener, service/container, Nginx configuration, and host firewall/ACL before selecting the smallest safe restriction. No origin configuration was available to back up, and no origin mutation was attempted.

No exact production mutation command can responsibly be supplied from this evidence: the listener's Nginx response is known, but its configuration, upstream, firewall manager, and whether the intended authenticated access is local-only or tailnet-ACL-based are unknown. Guessing a config path or adding a firewall rule could disrupt unrelated service or leave the endpoint exposed. The exact unblock is: have the tailnet administrator authorize the designated operator account for Tailscale SSH to `100.69.211.43`; then run the read-only discovery commands below in that host session before preparing/backing up the actual configuration.

## Read-only discovery commands for the authorized host session

```sh
hostname; id
sudo ss -ltnp '( sport = :8000 )'
sudo nginx -T
sudo systemctl --no-pager --type=service --state=running
sudo nft list ruleset
```

These commands identify the actual listener/fronting configuration and active network policy. Before any subsequent change, copy each file actually selected for editing to a sibling named with a UTC timestamp and `.bak` suffix; apply only an approved loopback bind, authenticated Nginx location, or verified Tailscale ACL restriction. Then verify from an untrusted network that port 8000 is non-routable or returns 401, and repeat the four public-route probes recorded below.

## Commands run and evidence

1. `hostname; id; ...; tailscale status --peers=false; ip route get 100.69.211.43`
   - Output: `brandon-compute`; `uid=1000(hd2admin)`; Tailscale status listed `100.106.6.22 brandon-compute`; route: `100.69.211.43 dev tailscale0 ... src 100.106.6.22`.
2. `curl -sS -D - --max-time 8 -o /dev/null http://100.69.211.43:8000/`
   - Output: `HTTP/1.1 200 OK`; `Server: nginx`; no `WWW-Authenticate` header.
3. `timeout 12 ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=5 -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no -o UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null hd2admin@100.69.211.43 'hostname; id; sudo -n true ...'`
   - Output: `tailscale: tailnet policy does not permit you to SSH as user "hd2admin"`; connection closed by port 22; exit 255.
4. `for port in 22 8000 80 443; do timeout 2 bash -c "</dev/tcp/100.69.211.43/$port" ...; done`
   - Output: all four ports reported open; this is a TCP reachability check, not proof of authorization or service identity.
5. `for p in https://agents.elevatesci.com/ https://agents.elevatesci.com/embed/ https://agents.elevatesci.com/api/embed/ https://elevatesci.com/; do curl -sS -L -o /dev/null --max-time 12 -w '%{http_code} %{url_effective}\\n' "$p"; done`
   - Output: `401 https://agents.elevatesci.com/`; `200 https://agents.elevatesci.com/embed/`; `200 https://agents.elevatesci.com/api/embed/`; `200 https://elevatesci.com/`.
6. `git status --short` before writing this report
   - Output: pre-existing unrelated untracked paths `core-distro-saas-clean` and `firstmate`; no staged files were reported.

